#import "../macros.typ": *

The ten classes below are the 1PI functions of ghost number 0 with $sum_j dim Phi_j <= 4$. Functions with an external $b^a$ are tree level, and those with an external $agh^a$ follow from the $srcA$ functions, because by the ghost equation @eq:linear-ids $Gamma$ depends on $agh^a$ and $srcA^mu_a$ only through the combination @eq:rhotilde. Functions with an external sterile component $chi_f$ have no one-loop diagram (@sec:g0_FF). Each class gives $N_0$ of every diagram and, by @eq:m-Z1, the vertex function of $delta S^((1))$, which is then written as a local functional in $d$ dimensions.

*What is kept.* External momenta and Lorentz indices are $d$-dimensional, and nothing is projected: the poles keep their evanescent parts, which enter $delta S^((1))$ and, through $bop_d delta S^((1))$, the pole checks of @sec:g1. Fermion loops couple only to $bar(A)$ and have four-dimensional numerators (@eq:phys-prop): they give barred tensors. Gauge, ghost and Higgs loops have $d$-dimensional numerators that are never split: they give #box[$d$-dimensional] covariant tensors. Hatted external momenta enter only through $hat(k) dot hat(p)$ in the denominators, two at a time, so that a hatted external structure outside these covariant tensors needs a superficial degree $dg(G) >= 2$. It occurs in the fermion loops of $A A$ and $H^dagger H$ as $hat(p)^2$, the two-point parts of $E^((r))_A$ and $E_H$ (@eq:evanescent). The $d$-dimensional form of $delta S^((1))$ as a whole is assembled in @sec:st-solution.

*Why $N_1 = 0$.* The tag $eta$ comes only from a closed trace $gmh^mu_(space mu)$, including the reduction $hat(k)^2 -> -2 eta k^2 \/ d$ (@eq:eta). In a ghost-number-0 diagram the fermion numerators contain no hatted matrix and no hatted loop momentum, and the bosonic numerators contain only unsplit objects. The only hatted loop components are those of $hat(k) dot hat(p)$ in the denominators, and they are contracted with hatted external momenta. No hatted trace closes inside the loop. Hence $N_1 = 0$, $Kop = Kop_eta$, and $delta S^((1))$ does not depend on where $d - 4$ is written as $-2 eta$ (@sec:m-K).
